  "body": [
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "A builder in Bangalore once poured three slabs with a 1400 x 1400 mm opening, confident it matched the lift he’d been quoted. It did — that was the cabin figure. The shaft needed 1700 x 1650 mm. ",
        {
          "tag": "a",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "strong",
              "children": [
                "Elevator dimensions "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/"
          }
        },
        "get confused like this constantly, and the correction always costs more than the original decision. BG Elevators can help clarify the required dimensions before construction begins."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "About six lift dimensions genuinely matter here, and once you know what each one describes, reading a supplier drawing stops being guesswork. Here’s what every measurement means, which ones the building has to deliver, and where people usually get caught out. BG Elevators can help you understand the measurements and plan the required space correctly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Why Brochure Numbers Aren’t the Numbers You Need"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-1"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Marketing material quotes capacity and cabin area because that’s what buyers ask about. The elevator dimensions your builder needs are different: clear internal shaft, pit depth, overhead, and structural load points."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Those two sets of elevator dimensions are never the same. Between cabin and shaft sit guide rails, brackets, the car frame, the door operator, counterweight running space and safety clearances — 200 to 350 mm on each axis depending on the machine. Ask for the shaft general arrangement drawing, not the leaflet."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Elevator Dimensions That Decide the Whole Design"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-2"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Six figures do most of the work:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "ol",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Clear internal shaft (width x depth): the finished hole, wall face to wall face."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Clear cabin (width x depth): usable floor inside the car."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Clear door opening: the width you actually walk or wheel through."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Pit depth: finished ground floor level down to the pit floor."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Overhead / headroom: top finished floor level to the underside of the shaft roof."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Total travel: bottom to top landing, which drives rope or ram length."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Cabin height, door height and machine footprint all follow from these. If a quotation gives you capacity and nothing else, you haven’t received a specification yet."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Shaft Dimensions: The Hole in the Building"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-3"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "h3",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Plan Size and Clearances"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-4"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Shaft dimensions are stated as clear internal size after plaster and finishes, and that last part trips people up. A 1600 mm rough opening with 15 mm plaster each side gives 1570 mm clear, and if the lift needed 1600 mm, the rails no longer fit."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Where the shaft is a steel structure rather than masonry, the clear dimension is measured between inside faces of the frame, with cladding outside it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h3",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Plumb and Squareness"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-5"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Tolerance matters as much as size. A shaft should be plumb within a few millimetres over its full height and square in plan. Out-of-plumb shafts are the commonest reason equipment arrives on site and then waits. Errors accumulate as the shaft rises, so check plumb at every floor rather than at the end."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Cabin Dimensions and Usable Space"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-6"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Cabin dimensions determine what the lift can actually do. A 900 x 900 mm car takes two adults comfortably and a third awkwardly. Put a wheelchair in it and you’ll find it’s too shallow to turn."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "For accessibility, roughly 1100 mm wide by 1400 mm deep is the practical minimum, because a wheelchair user can enter facing forward and reverse out without turning. If a stretcher must fit, depth is the number to watch, and the 2000 mm hospital-style lifts use is rarely feasible at home."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Cabin height is worth confirming too. Standard is 2100–2300 mm, and a dropped false ceiling quietly takes 80–100 mm off it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Pit Depth and Headroom"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-7"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "These two elevator dimensions decide which drive types are available to you before price enters the conversation."
      ]
    },
