                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "tag": "td",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "tag": "p",
                      "children": [
                        "1200–1600 mm"
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "tag": "td",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "tag": "p",
                      "children": [
                        "3800–4200 mm"
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "tag": "td",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "tag": "p",
                      "children": [
                        "Larger commercial installations"
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Measure available overhead on site rather than trusting a drawing, especially in an existing building where a beam may intrude. Pit depth needs the same reality check: if the water table is high or the plot sits low, waterproofing and a sump belong in the specification."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Door Opening: The Most Underestimated Measurement"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-8"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Clear door opening is the width between panels when fully open, always smaller than the cabin width. Typical values run 700, 800 and 900 mm."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "ul",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "700 mm works for walking passengers and nothing else."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "800 mm is the practical minimum for wheelchair access."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "900 mm suits stretchers, furniture and busier buildings."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Door height is usually 2000–2100 mm, which matters when you’re carrying a wardrobe. Confirm the door type early too: telescopic and centre-opening panels need different headboard widths for the same clear opening, and that changes the elevator dimensions your shaft has to provide."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Standard Elevator Size by Capacity"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-9"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Capacity in kilograms is the usual starting point for elevator dimensions, and each step up has a footprint cost."
      ]
    },
