    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Treat these as indicative. ",
        {
          "tag": "a",
          "children": [
            {
              "tag": "strong",
              "children": [
                "Elevator size "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/services"
          }
        },
        "varies between manufacturers at identical capacity, sometimes by 100 mm or more, because rail positions and door mechanisms differ. Never build a shaft to elevator dimensions from an article, including this one. Build it to the stamped drawing for the model you’ve bought."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "How to Read Elevator Dimensions on a Supplier Drawing"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-10"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "A proper general arrangement drawing shows a plan at cabin level, a section through the full travel, and a schedule of loads. Check five things about the elevator measurements before signing it off."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "ol",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Is the shaft dimension marked “clear” or “structural”? They differ by the finish thickness."
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Does the section show pit depth from finished floor level, not from the plinth?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Are the reaction loads at the pit floor and shaft head given in kN, so your engineer can design for them?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Do the landing door opening sizes match at every floor?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "tag": "li",
          "children": [
            "Does the travel match your actual floor-to-floor heights, which are often not identical?"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "That last one catches people out in independent houses, where the ground floor is often taller than the floors above. Give the supplier real elevator measurements from site, not the architect’s intended figures from three years ago."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h2",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Frequently Asked Questions"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "h3",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "What is the minimum shaft size for a small home lift?"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-12"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Roughly 1000 x 1000 mm of clear shaft supports a two-person screw-driven car of about 750 x 750 mm. That’s usable for two standing adults but not for a wheelchair. For accessible use, plan for a shaft near 1600 x 1500 mm, which gives a 1100 x 1400 mm cabin."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h3",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "How much bigger is the shaft than the cabin?"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-13"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Generally 200 to 350 mm on each dimension, covering guide rails, brackets, the car frame, door operator and running clearances. The exact allowance depends on drive type and manufacturer, so confirm both elevator dimensions on the same drawing rather than adding a rule of thumb yourself."
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "h3",
      "children": [
        {
          "tag": "strong",
          "children": [
            "Do elevator dimensions change with speed?"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "id": "section-14"
      }
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "children": [
        "Plan size stays broadly the same, but faster lifts need deeper pits and more overhead for buffers and safety clearances. A 1.0 m/s machine typically wants more headroom than a 0.5 m/s unit of identical capacity, which matters if your terrace level is already fixed."
      ]
    },
    {
